#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163 :topic/math-academy-id 1167 :topic/title "Index Notation for Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765 :topic/math-academy-id 148 :topic/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices"}] :topic/id #uuid "d0f342d9-c70c-537f-9e04-f3b0beaf9c01" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129113 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128925 :question/id #uuid "674e7733-7a8e-5804-a867-82c74c583dcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-4687" :question/problem "Which of the following augmented matrices are in row echelon form?\n![](images/27/2701e789f5d3f6fc48fda87706add41ccb59c92d5bae4a76e84ac1abfdce6f07.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that an augmented matrix is in row echelon form if its coefficient matrix satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The first matrix is not in row echelon form since $a_{32} \\neq 0$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/d8/d8725937a4f5f1cfcd36cbe4871a872187df071b54c15d2e29d9b1e3c5f9b731.svg)\n\n- The second matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/5e/5e3142b1ad4bb219db5d5b144e47fe8d1fa8d1f92c300c6f60fc145cf9424900.svg)\n\n- The third matrix is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/93/9331fdbede7da49e2dbbc66aac0b3084b1c7acca1ac7e789eded304956a18500.svg)\n\n- The fourth matrix is not in row echelon form since, for example, the leading entry $a_{22}$ lies to the left of the leading entry $a_{13}$ (condition II is violated).\n![](images/76/76e7add6349e0b2fceddf094ac6e3e8cfd0bf1bb43a3fbee0c143b256b81f1c5.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Augmented Matrices in Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129114 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128926 :question/id #uuid "b6e4983b-c695-5944-9637-6d7547dae849" :question/math-academy-id "e-4688" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are in row echelon form?\n\n![](images/56/564d16a59313514424d24b8c247d8a3516bfa32188292d2cbc7fee7faf2f5744.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a matrix is in row echelon form if it satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- All the zero rows (rows with only zeros), if any, are placed at the *bottom* of the matrix.\n\n- Each leading entry of a row is in a column *to the right* of the leading entry of the row above it.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each matrix in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is not in row echelon form since the second row contains only zeros and it is not at the bottom of the matrix (condition I is violated).\n![](images/53/53977f4d27cf659f7d595afc4422b0c3009218fe836615eba5faf8a854124c6b.svg)\n\n- The matrix $B$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/40/40fea7849f3603441671253bb7bc96e56be9bab6a8ddcbc94320238a0e3f529a.svg)\n\n- The matrix $C$ is in row echelon form (both conditions are satisfied).\n![](images/08/08ed315816def9b28fb3918a2dd103a5008815fc6a07507b64c5c20572b1c88f.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Matrices in Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129115 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128927 :question/id #uuid "97237f8e-b317-5c86-86d1-8fd3cc83e66b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4689" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ given below.\nWhich entries of $A$ could be replaced by $0$ in order to get a matrix in row echelon form?\n![](images/aa/aad9e35c3e07e67260a7f3ce96da685be97fab68fe542d262be0ed4f0941f909.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that by replacing $a_{32}$, $a_{42}$, and $a_{43}$ with $0$, we get a matrix in row echelon form:\n![](images/f8/f89c32a7464b2eeb0f6db6a2c80feef98e8aa24d012b44695e7c7d24b91829ec.svg)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Operations That Would Transform a Matrix To Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129176 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128995 :question/id #uuid "7fd5c01f-c20e-5c47-b8d9-8cfbe634c14d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6166" :question/problem "What is the sum of the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows in the matrix $A,$ given below?\n![](images/85/8579b0afd584298a247c38af153d4e69945e73923e9605a168734c9250f3d18a.svg)" :question/worked-solution "The first non-zero entry of each row (from left to right) is the leading entry. \n\nTherefore, the leading entries of the $1$st, $3$rd and $4$th rows of $A$ are $-1$, $-4$ and $8$, respectively.\n\n![](images/11/11af9a5354d23586af399f9bfade0641e9bb10898068ea825755de5c645e3d9c.svg)\n\nSo, the answer is $-1+(-4)+8=3.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Leading Entries of a Matrix"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2083 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267 :topic/math-academy-id 1047 :topic/title "Solving Systems of Equations Using Back Substitution"}] :topic/title "Row Echelon Form"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050163 :topic/math-academy-id 1167 :topic/title "Index Notation for Matrices"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765 :topic/math-academy-id 148 :topic/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices"}] :topic/id #uuid "dc267e07-dc7a-5013-bcb3-616951f01683" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059771 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059580 :question/id #uuid "836f2f1b-5dd4-5dba-81cc-eb9ffe7db8d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-4693" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/62/627d12115d61ddfdedf9db08330269de2137a23abceada977d039e90e5b88675.png)\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_2 := R_2 +(- 4) \\cdot R_1$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_2:=R_2 + (-4) R_1$ means that we need to add $-4$ times the first row to the second row. \n\nCarrying out this row operation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4+(-4)(1) & 2+(-4)(2) & -1+(-4)(3) \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -13 \\\\ 7 & 12 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To a Coefficients Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059772 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059581 :question/id #uuid "2ff0a6f5-f3b2-5b39-8b69-1a0c969548cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-4694" :question/problem "Consider the augmented matrix $A,$ given by\n![](images/82/82729e1d34174d58f01547b58aa63db28ec68a4e1964ef5cd127a2167ed4a722.png) \n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ on $A?$" :question/worked-solution "The operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}]\\,{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 1 & 9\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Applying Row Operations To an Augmented Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059773 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059582 :question/id #uuid "a24c9621-793b-5c60-9c47-2b452f0faa2b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4695" :question/problem "Which elementary row operation could transform the matrix $A$ into the matrix $B$, where \n![](images/56/56fd7e343edf6c26f36718a30f899af6c8eaf0cad5f6b07c90ffa93c19df6346.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the $1$st and the $3$rd rows are the same in both matrices. The only row that has been changed is the $2$nd row. \n\nNow, applying the operation $R_2:=R_2+1 \\cdot R_1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+1\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 4+1\\cdot (-4) & 1+1\\cdot (-2) & 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the transformation could be done by the operation \n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+1\\cdot R_1\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2:=R_2+R_1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform One Matrix Into Another"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059774 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059583 :question/id #uuid "f8417bbc-b748-519b-a997-fbb4aed755eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4696" :question/problem "Given that\n![](images/59/59ee8e91d058e347238320a2bd5496630f33534564f67f901d2ddf89000f0108.png) which elementary row operation could make $a_{22}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "In the given matrix, we have $a_{22}=5$ and we want to perform an elementary row operation that makes $a_{22}=0.$\n\n- We *cannot* make $a_{22}=0$ by switching two rows, because there are no rows with $0$ as the second entry.\n\n- We *cannot* multiply the second row by $0,$ because we are only allowed to multiply rows by *nonzero* constants.\n\n- However, we *can* add a multiple of another row to the $2$nd row to make $a_{22}=0.$\n\nLooking at the first row, we see that $a_{12}=1.$ If we add $-5$ times this entry to $a_{22},$ we will get $a_{22}=0$ as desired. So, the elementary row operation that we need to use is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1.\n$$\n\nApplying this operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5)\\cdot {R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1+(-5)\\cdot 3 & 5+(-5)\\cdot 1 & 6+(-5)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -16 & 0 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired elemetary row operation is\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 + (-5) \\cdot R_1,\n$$\n\nor simply\n\n$$\nR_2 := R_2 -5 R_1.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Appropriate Row Operation To Transform a Given Entry to a Desired Value"}] :topic/math-academy-id 149 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048275 :topic/math-academy-id 2015 :topic/title "Matrices Over the Complex Numbers"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050527 :topic/math-academy-id 3029 :topic/title "Creating Rows or Columns Containing Zeros Using Gaussian Elimination"}] :topic/title "Elementary Row Operations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050722 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049144 :topic/math-academy-id 1255 :topic/title "Cartesian Equations of Parametric Curves"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049570 :topic/math-academy-id 843 :topic/title "Equations of Circles"}] :topic/id #uuid "614ee6b8-9a8a-566b-9824-c4103b2e358e" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087554 :question/id #uuid "b798cfbb-89ac-5d31-adb5-5c43bd483226" :question/math-academy-id "e-5374" :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](images/15/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at the Origin"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087555 :question/id #uuid "140fc720-22c0-5832-afae-c255144291d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5375" :question/problem "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087784 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087612 :question/id #uuid "4ffaeeac-95a0-598a-9880-166d03f810ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-6779" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](images/4b/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing Part of a Circle"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087786 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087616 :question/id #uuid "8bff424d-8f2e-528b-acf0-12c33958a132" :question/math-academy-id "e-6817" :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of a Parametrized Circle"}] :topic/math-academy-id 806 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048450 :topic/math-academy-id 1947 :topic/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049512 :topic/math-academy-id 3688 :topic/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Circles"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049628 :topic/math-academy-id 3372 :topic/title "Surfaces of Revolution"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049672 :topic/math-academy-id 2746 :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Ellipses"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050104 :topic/math-academy-id 1401 :topic/title "Outward-Pointing Unit Normal Vectors in 2D"}] :topic/title "Parametric Equations of Circles"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050765 :topic/_next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048678 :topic/math-academy-id 2225 :topic/title "Introduction to Systems of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896 :topic/math-academy-id 861 :topic/title "Introduction to Matrices"}] :topic/id #uuid "64dbabd7-08e6-5435-b006-ab4997312f47" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059767 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059576 :question/id #uuid "b2196eb1-6691-549b-950a-a9bab6863a70" :question/math-academy-id "e-4683" :question/problem "What is the coefficient matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/14/14671e805c18814b31d0c65d0e53c60aae1cf52c60ad8dd146d8a3f4bc823915.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/e0/e0318c549ab4aa25bf2decb41e45e296406d699706c03aff7ea44724d20edece.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n\t\\matrix {\n\t\t1 & -2 \\\n\t\t0 & 2 \n\t}\n\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059768 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059577 :question/id #uuid "b5967dd6-bdf9-5cd0-9639-ec2816d04256" :question/math-academy-id "e-4684" :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/a5/a5abba041a61cad76d43d5bb13d0fd4e873eb2daf2282e8d9828b8ab79fe2ba0.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $x,y,z$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/2f/2f9ae37920ec528a700715658900bbbf3ec4c855767bf06f79d044f488a8bbf2.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left\\lbrack\n\t\\matrix {\n\t\t1 & 1 & -3 \\\n\t\t-7 & -1 & 1 \\\n                2 & 1 & -2 \n\t}\n\t\\right\\rbrack\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix  is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n![](images/af/af1499610c0c312a1f5c50d99967a004ae6b3042c3a88ca20a86f922b28aaaff.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a System of Linear Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059769 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059578 :question/id #uuid "05942b5f-c165-59c6-8b67-24bc8dd40cca" :question/math-academy-id "e-4685" :question/problem "What would be the system of equations for the following augmented matrix?\n\n![](images/4d/4d90e81043121495ae8bb7742400b2c9e705cb7d706f553a6ab2aec44b35d017.png)" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix consists of two parts: the coefficient matrix and the column of constants.\n\nThe coefficient matrix represents the coefficients in the linear system, while the column of constants represents the constants on the right-hand sides of the equations.\n\nUsing the variables $x$ and $y,$ then, the corresponding system of equations is\n![](images/3e/3e7f783023405be3ea79a740998fefac3a6ea909e9903f5ea300a9234ae63003.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the System of Equations Associated With an Augmented Matrix"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059770 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059579 :question/id #uuid "161a4327-2356-5779-92a6-8495cfffefb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4686" :question/problem "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n![](images/d8/d89417b7eb2d000bbd81fcf4a89ca7eaa5529fc56d9b41bf6e8bb237d2eb9e16.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we organize the system so that the variables are ordered $a,b,c,d$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient:\n\n![](images/93/93874fa93cfd9ef3df1492bbc35056c2381157740d8ca26b475474faf54ee4d1.png)\n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables:\n\n$$\n\\left[\\begin{array}{ccc|c}-1 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n![](images/83/8356d9148bc06d76a5aee41e4a07651c43d94b2496d9542eb8b6e01ee52ea161.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Augmented Matrix of a Linear System With Variables Other Than x, y, z"}] :topic/math-academy-id 148 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048980 :topic/math-academy-id 2083 :topic/title "Row Echelon Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049120 :topic/math-academy-id 149 :topic/title "Elementary Row Operations"}] :topic/title "Systems of Equations as Augmented Matrices"}]}
